WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.520
Monash Automation is a student-led engineering team here at Monash University. We focus on a

00:14.520 --> 00:20.520
variety of projects that include robotics, AI and automation. The heart of Monash Automation

00:20.520 --> 00:26.920
has always been in robotics and manufacturing and so what better place to push the boundaries

00:26.920 --> 00:32.780
of robotics in than the emerging field of bipedal robotics. The project Waddle asks

00:32.780 --> 00:39.180
a simple question, what does it take to design, build and control a bipedal robot from scratch?

00:39.180 --> 00:44.100
Waddle is our team's adorable little mascot brought to life by our talented design team.

00:44.100 --> 00:49.400
Waddle is a penguin that sports Monash Automation's signature colours and appears all around our

00:49.400 --> 00:56.720
lab as stickers, on our merch and even as 3D printed little figurines. Waddle has been

00:56.720 --> 01:01.260
developed from the ground up by us. The team is responsible for designing the robot's

01:01.260 --> 01:07.420
mechanical structure, joint geometry, actuator integration, bodywork and supporting hardware.

01:07.420 --> 01:11.720
Custom components are developed in-house in CAD before being machined, 3D printed and

01:11.720 --> 01:16.980
fabricated in-house, allowing the team to rapidly prototype, test and refine the design.

01:16.980 --> 01:21.540
This has created a significantly deeper engineering challenge as every mechanical decision influences

01:21.540 --> 01:26.340
the rest of the system. Waddle represents a major shift from earlier versions. Previous

01:26.340 --> 01:31.720
designs followed more of an outside in approach. This year we flipped it around and started

01:31.720 --> 01:37.840
from the inside out. Starting with the frame and the training simulation required to make

01:37.840 --> 01:39.940
it work.

01:39.940 --> 01:48.760
Waddle is being virtually simulated inside NVIDIA's Isaac Sim, a robotic simulator that

01:48.760 --> 01:53.960
allows us to test a digital version of the robot. It acts as a virtual sandbox where

01:53.960 --> 01:56.340
we can test everything from Waddle's joints and sensors, to the robot's body and body

01:56.340 --> 02:03.400
sensors, to its motion, actuators and interactions with its environment. Built on top of Isaac

02:03.400 --> 02:09.960
Sim is a framework called Isaac Lab which helps us train, test and develop our robot's

02:09.960 --> 02:16.800
behaviour. Through a process known as reinforcement learning, Waddle develops its own movements

02:16.800 --> 02:22.100
through trial and error. We created its digital robot model, designed its walking motion,

02:22.100 --> 02:26.020
established a range of rewards and penalties and then allowed the neural network to be

02:26.020 --> 02:32.200
able to learn from those parameters. By running thousands of simulated Waddles in parallel,

02:32.200 --> 02:36.800
the network can learn from millions of simulated steps and experiences.

02:36.800 --> 02:41.320
Building a sufficiently accurate robot inside the simulator is crucial. We need to account

02:41.320 --> 02:47.820
for properties such as friction, mass, motor strength, sensor behaviour and control delays.

02:47.820 --> 02:52.680
Waddle's digital model needs to represent its physical counterpart closely enough that

02:52.680 --> 02:54.960
behaviours learned in simulation have a realistic chance on working together.

02:54.960 --> 02:55.960
Waddle's digital model needs to represent its physical counterpart closely enough that

02:55.960 --> 02:59.000
behaviours learned in simulation have a realistic chance on working together. The reverse process,

02:59.000 --> 03:04.280
real-to-sim, is just as important, because measurements and observations from the physical

03:04.280 --> 03:08.980
robot must be used to continually improve the simulation.

03:08.980 --> 03:14.020
So far, Waddle has achieved forward walking in simulation, although we are still working

03:14.020 --> 03:21.180
to make the movement smoother and more stable.

03:21.180 --> 03:25.340
Sharon AI is an Australian company that provides high-performance computing infrastructure,

03:25.340 --> 03:30.840
specifically designed for AI and other demanding workloads. Sharon AI gives organisations

03:30.840 --> 03:36.520
access to powerful GPU compute, without requiring them to purchase and maintain all of the hardware

03:36.520 --> 03:37.520
themselves.

03:37.520 --> 03:44.200
So, a hyperscaler sits between the data centre and whoever is using the compute. So, Sharon

03:44.200 --> 03:49.240
AI, they're a sovereign Australian hyperscaler, so they deal with local Australian data centres,

03:49.240 --> 03:53.800
they get all the hardware, deploy it into the data centre and then manage that and then

03:53.800 --> 03:55.180
rent out the hardware. For us, we're very, very happy with the fact that we're able to

03:55.180 --> 03:55.260
operate all of these technologies in the correct applications. It's a fascinating experience,

03:55.260 --> 03:58.740
we have access to a virtual machine with an NVIDIA L40S

03:59.020 --> 04:01.220
in their Melbourne data center deployment.

04:01.460 --> 04:05.180
What that has allowed us to do is we can scale from consumer grade

04:05.500 --> 04:09.340
graphics cards up to proper enterprise grade graphics card.

04:09.380 --> 04:12.580
Each environment must calculate models, joints, motors,

04:12.940 --> 04:16.580
balance, collisions and contact with this environment.

04:17.140 --> 04:21.860
Across a full training run, this can involve millions of simulated interactions,

04:22.300 --> 04:25.020
which makes the process highly computationally demanding.

04:25.820 --> 04:28.780
Available GPU memory can restrict the size of an experiment,

04:29.140 --> 04:31.980
and a single training run may occupy a computer that is also needed

04:31.980 --> 04:35.060
for design, programming or other university work.

04:35.420 --> 04:37.580
The main benefit for us is just scalability.

04:37.860 --> 04:39.940
So it's flexibility as to how much we need.

04:39.940 --> 04:41.660
So we can do local testing here.

04:41.660 --> 04:45.300
We have access to a DGX Spark from NVIDIA as well as our 4090s.

04:45.300 --> 04:50.580
What Sharon AI lets us do is scale this up as needed for our projects.

04:50.780 --> 04:54.900
So we might test a project locally here on sort of a Jetson, Raspberry Pi,

04:55.260 --> 04:57.540
just a consumer grade graphics card.

04:58.020 --> 05:00.500
But then we realized that actually we'd get a lot of benefit

05:00.900 --> 05:05.140
by deploying this true enterprise grade hardware, by scaling up the number of GPUs

05:05.140 --> 05:08.180
and the amount of compute that we can actually throw at the problem.

05:08.460 --> 05:13.060
So we might be running something that needs eight GPUs for 12 hours,

05:13.340 --> 05:15.420
but then for the rest of the month, we don't need that.

05:15.420 --> 05:17.340
We might need one GPU to sustain that.

05:17.340 --> 05:18.740
We might need no GPUs.

05:18.740 --> 05:25.060
So having that scalability and that flexibility is a really important part of how we can optimize our usage

05:25.260 --> 05:26.380
of compute.

05:26.380 --> 05:29.460
More compute does not automatically produce a better robot,

05:29.460 --> 05:34.780
but it allows us to test ideas more thoroughly and reach useful results much faster.
